[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a well-known result in algebraic geometry / algebraic topology. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124manswer is YES.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about the proof carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. For $k = \mathbb{C}$: A smooth quasi-projective variety $X$ over $\mathbb{C}$[0m[38;2;124;124;124m— its $\mathbb{C}$-points form a complex manifold (since smooth + quasi-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mprojective over $\mathbb{C}$ gives a smooth complex analytic space, which is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcomplex manifold). Complex manifolds are triangulable (by Lojasiewicz's theorem [0m[38;2;124;124;124mor by the fact that complex algebraic varieties are triangulable). Actually, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmore directly: smooth quasi-projective complex varieties are triangulable, hence[0m[38;2;124;124;124mhomeomorphic to a simplicial complex, hence homotopy equivalent to a CW complex.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. For $k = \mathbb{R}$: Real points of smooth varieties. This is more subtle. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mReal algebraic varieties (even smooth ones) can have singularities in the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msemialgebraic sense... Actually, $X(\mathbb{R})$ for smooth $X$ is a smooth real[0m[38;2;124;124;124manalytic space, but it can be non-compact and have various topological types. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHowever, by Nash-Tognoli type results or by semialgebraic triangulation, real [0m
[38;2;124;124;124malgebraic sets are triangulable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think more carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $\mathbb{C}$: [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $X$ smooth quasi-projective over $\mathbb{C}$ → $X^{an}$ is a complex manifold[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(by GAGA-type analytification, smooth algebraic variety gives smooth analytic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspace = complex manifold).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Complex manifolds are triangulable? Actually, not all complex manifolds are [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mknown to be triangulable in general, but complex algebraic varieties ARE [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtriangulable. This is a theorem by Lojasiewicz (1964) or by Hironaka. Actually, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe triangulability of semianalytic sets and subanalytic sets is due to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mLojasiewicz. Complex algebraic varieties (even singular ones) are triangulable —[0m[38;2;124;124;124mthis is a classical result.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- More precisely: every complex algebraic variety is triangulable (homeomorphic [0m[38;2;124;124;124mto a simplicial complex). This is due to Lojasiewicz for semialgebraic/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124msemianalytic sets, and algebraic varieties over $\mathbb{C}$ are semianalytic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(actually, they're subanalytic and even semianalytic in projective space).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me be more careful. The triangulability of complex algebraic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mvarieties: [0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- Hironaka showed that algebraic varieties over $\mathbb{C}$ can be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtriangulated. Actually, the standard reference is that complex algebraic sets [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare triangulable. This follows from the fact that they are semianalytic sets (in[0m[38;2;124;124;124maffine space) and Lojasiewicz's triangulation theorem for semianalytic sets.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $\mathbb{R}$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $X(\mathbb{R})$ for $X$ a real algebraic variety is a semialgebraic set (by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mTarski-Seidenberg, or more directly, it's defined by polynomial equations in $[0m
